(VAL-19: conditional `value` returns — the factorial pattern (`If ...
 return a value, <expr>. Otherwise ...`) in a function whose `To` line has
 no `Return`.
 **This probe was refreshed on 2026-08-21: vox #43 (0.4.8) fixed both
 halves of what it used to record.** Under 0.4.7 returning a text literal
 from a frame whose parameter was a number printed stable garbage
 (4210906) mis-tagged as `Number (dynamic)`, and the reverse direction
 segfaulted (Discrepancy 1, `D1.vox`). Today each branch hands back its
 own runtime tag: the `Otherwise` branch's text literal comes back as
 `fallback` / `Text (dynamic)`, and nothing crashes. LANGUAGE.md agrees —
 it now says "Conditional `value` returns work" (:2626-2639), where 0.4.7
 called it a limitation. The conditional-value-PARAMETER half (void
 return) worked before and still works — also shown here.
 Ran: VOX_CORE_PATH=/home/josj/scr/english/vox/coreasm /home/josj/scr/english/vox/target/release/vox VAL-19.vox -o p && ./p
 expected output:
   kept
   Text (dynamic)
   fallback
   Text (dynamic)
   num
   not num
)
To label with a value called v.
  If v is a text, return a value, v.
  Otherwise, return a value, "fallback".

a value called kept is label of "kept".
print kept.
print kept's type.
a value called lost is label of 9.
